417] [17592186240697 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240697 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240697 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240697 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240697 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cefc758a-5b74-5f60-8c85-a8cfa0ef9113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/49/494e360a4c22d96feb811de6ee507996a11be65ee346d837debcb927c0d28434.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,2)$ with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,2)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "face32c2-838a-5a8c-a2dc-dfdf5610f42d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241165 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b47425b-d1be-54fd-a4d5-ddff6578df69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241172 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300280 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300280 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300280 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301369 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301369 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301369 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301369 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302354 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345389 #edb/ref 1080 "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]" #edb/ref 17592186045417