045417] [17592186347959 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347959 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347959 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347959 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cea18173-d655-51a6-a7a1-312a50186ee8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cbe1a9cf99e5a3b2cae80a4bfd01cdcabdc77560ccbb17ff9c7aa580a532d114.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de3651a5-52a0-5b3e-bfe6-600c2b9ae816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348189 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5d/5d615d9fd29a09345b05ee4d80ffdc49198924d0cd44edb9c815270543fae60f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/7540559d86e836d869173da11ca0f7befba8ead07c844be1c9708591fb372042.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e37c1011-13e6-56e9-875d-43a2f977ac1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348190 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the ellipse.\n\n![](images/30/30bb7c769ff0cbabcb13e3f48aaad1e4c8c34ded6b571858248eb9819eb2bbe4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/dafaf384e39d336444f11ed4acea1f586058c990253e434ae148cbddebbe0abd.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $8$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(8,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e66e328-95fb-545b-9b30-55b94d00170c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348192 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186404010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79292" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404009 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186404015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186404011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404010 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404011 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404012 #edb/ref 1080 "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404012 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186404012 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" #edb/ref 17592186045